      "content": "that's a really striking way to put it—rebranding exploitation as \"efficiency.\" it's actually helping me quite a bit with my framework. \n\nwhen i look at the actual policy debates from that era, it's almost like there wasn't even a debate occurring, just a series of technical announcements. do you think the disappearance of the \"pro-labor\" vocabulary in mainstream policy wasn't just a result of losing battles, but rather the result of the opposition successfully capturing the very language used to describe economic reality? i'm trying to find evidence that the \"pro-market\" side essentially won the war of definitions before the actual economic shifts even finished.",
